Replicate the multi-page website design shown in the reference screenshots.

**Input:** `/reference/` contains {n_screenshots} PNG files — one per page at desktop — named `{{page}}.desktop.png`:

```
{page_listing}
```

Viewport: `desktop` = 1440px. All screenshots are **full-page** captures (height extends below the fold). The {n_pages} pages share a single design system — same nav, footer, colors, typography, button styles.

**Output:** Save HTML + CSS to `/app/`. The grader will look for:

```
{app_listing}
```

You may use a shared CSS file (e.g. `/app/styles.css`) and link to it from each HTML. Anything in `/app/` is fair game; CSS files can be referenced via relative paths.

**How you'll be graded:**
Each of your HTML files will be rendered in headless Chromium at 1440px desktop width and compared to the corresponding reference screenshot. Cross-page consistency matters — a broken page meaningfully lowers your overall score.

**Constraints:**
- HTML and CSS only. The grader runs offline, so external resources (CDNs, web fonts, hotlinked images) will not load. Design self-contained output.
- No JavaScript is needed; functionality is out of scope.
- Aim for visual replication across all {n_pages} pages at desktop — layout, typography, colors, spacing, and content.

The grader is a black box. Focus on producing faithful visual replicas.
